  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nNo major changes made to the extended periods. A Bermuda High \nwill linger off the eastern seaboard, which will continue to \nspread southerly flow and low level moisture into the Southeast. \nBy the time the long term period picks up on Sat morning, \ndewpoints will be in the low to mid 70s and precipitable water \nvalues will be between 1.75-2.2 inches across the majority of the \narea. Meanwhile, a shortwave trough will be advancing east- \nnortheast from the middle Mississippi River Valley region towards \nthe lower Midwest. As this shortwave progresses, a weakening \nfrontal boundary will be moving southward through central \nTennessee and approaching the north Georgia border. Scattered to \nnumerous thunderstorms are expected to continue through much of \nnorth Georgia to the south of this front.\n\nWarm, moist southerly flow will help high temperatures Saturday rise \ninto the upper 80s to low 90s in spite of increased cloud/precip \ncoverage. The combination of diurnal destabilization and the \nboundary progressing further south will promote a further increase \nin thunderstorm coverage across the area by late Saturday morning \nthrough the evening hours. PoPs at this time are forecast to range \nfrom 80-90% across north Georgia (closer to the frontal boundary)\nand 50-80% over Central Georgia. Given the high PWATs, stronger \nstorms that develop will be efficient rainfall producers and could\nlead to localized flooding concerns, most likely in areas to the \nnorth of the Fall Line. Shear ahead of the front will become \nnegligible as the front continues to weaken. Considering this and \na nearly saturated atmospheric column, the threat for organized \nsevere weather will be low. However, with ample diurnal \ninstability, a few storms will be capable of becoming strong and \ncapable of producing frequent lightning and gusty winds.\n\nSaturday night, the remnant boundary is anticipated to stall near \nthe I-85 corridor, with some showers expected to linger in the \nvicinity. With lesser precipitation and cloud coverage on Sunday, \ntemperatures are expected to increase by a couple of degrees, \nwarming into the low to mid 90s in all but the far northeastern \nmountains. With dewpoints remaining in the 70s, heat index values \nwill climb into the low to mid 100s in areas south of I-85 and in \nthe upper 90s in areas to the north in the peak heating hours. \nScattered afternoon pulse thunderstorms are expected on Sunday, \ncloser to the remnant boundary. There will be little change in the \nairmass during the early parts of next week, with humid conditions \nand above normal temperatures combining for heat indices exceeding \n100 across the majority of the area Monday through Wednesday. In an \nunstable airmass but in the absence of a surface forcing mechanism, \nisolated to widely scattered thunderstorms are expected each of \nthose afternoons.",
